  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nBroad high pressure along the East Coast will become centered\nsouth of New England today through Thursday. This will provide\nfair weather while parts of today will feature more clouds than\nsun with more in the way of sunshine Thursday. As high pressure\nwill be centered south of the region, temperatures at 925 mb\nwill climb to around +15C today and +19C Thursday. While winds\nwill be light, model soundings show mixing to at least 925 mb\nwill be possible both days which will translate to highs in the\nlow 70s today and upper 70s Thursday. Light winds and skies\ntrending mostly clear tonight will likely lead to valley fog.\nSteady SW winds and a warm air mass in place will lead to a mild\nnight Thursday night with lows failing to drop below 60 degrees\nacross much of the area.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA shortwave and attendant cold front will cross the region Friday \nafternoon-night. Temperatures are more uncertain owing to potential \ncloud cover and precipitation, but guidance has slowed down the \nfront enough to suggest one more warm day in store, with 850 mb \ntemps pushing 13-15 C along with warm SW surface winds, which \nsupports highs well into the mid-upper 70s. With a frontal passage \naround/just after peak heating, cannot rule out some heavier \ndownpours and convection with around 500 J/Kg of CAPE indicated on \nmultiple sources. PWATs also surge to 1.5-1.8\"+ depending on model \nof choice, which is 2-3 SD above climatology, indicating the \npotential for heavy downpours. A very progressive system looks to \ncap precipitation totals however, with only a 30% of exceeding 0.5\" \nof rain for the forecast area on the ECMWF ensemble. Overall does \nnot look like a particularly high impact event as of now, but \nsometimes stronger dynamics this time of year can compensate for \nmore marginal instability/moisture.\n\nSpeaking of, very strong CAA behind the cold front with 850 mb temps \nnosediving to 2-5 C by Saturday evening, which will be quite a \nairmass change. Highs in the 50s-60s this weekend will be \nrepresentative of a cooler shift from this week.\n\nVery strong agreement on global guidance in another dynamic trough \nand attendant cold front approaching the region late Sunday then \ncrossing through Monday. Similar to the first wave, a bit of a \nglancing blow with the surface low and attendant vort max well to \nthe north up in Quebec. This keeps the system progressive with only \na weak signal for quick-hitting precipitation along the cold front, \nbut this may even be a mostly dry frontal passage depending on how \nit all shakes out, as ensemble support for rainfall is not high \ncurrently. An even stronger cold punch is possible behind this \nsecond front with 850 mb temps going negative, which at a glance \ncould spell quite chilly and crisp conditions for a few days.",
